===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*terh₁-}}
From {{uder|en|la|trītus}} "worn out," a form of the verb {{m|la|terō||I wear away, wear out}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er}}

# Often in reference to a word or phrase: used so many times that it is [[commonplace]], or no longer [[interesting]] or [[effective]]; worn out, [[hackneyed]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1897|author=W. B. Kimberly|title=History of West Australia : A Narrative of Her Past together with Biographies of Her Leading Men
|passage=It is a '''trite''' saying in a young country that anyone starting out in life with the determination to become wealthy will have his wish gratified.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Anthony Bergin|chapter=The High Seas Regime – Pacific Trends and Developments|editors=[[w:James Crawford (jurist)|James Crawford]]; Donald R. Rothwell|title=The Law of the Sea in the Asian Pacific Region: Developments and Prospects|location=Dordrecht|publisher=w:Martinus Nijhoff Publishers|year=1994|page=183|isbn=978-0-7923-2742-4|passage=It is '''trite''' history&nbsp;– and '''trite''' law&nbsp;– to say that the law of the sea since that time [World War II] reflects a history of coastal State expansion.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2007|author=Danielle Corsetto|title=Girls with Slingshots: 267|url=http://www.girlswithslingshots.com/comic/gws267/
|passage=McPedro the cactus: How to woo a woman! On yehr fahrst date, don’t bring her cut flowers! That’s inhumane! And '''trite'''!}}
#*{{quote-web|en|date=4-3-2025|author=Marina Hyde|url=https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2025/mar/04/meghan-markle-art-political-cultural-duchess-of-sussex-with-love-meghan|work=The Guardian|title=It’s With Love, Meghan – not just a TV show but a landmark piece of art. And not in a good way|text=This show is sensationally absurd and '''trite''', and if you watch it, you know it.}}
# {{lb|en|legal}} So well {{l|en|established#Adjective}} as to be beyond debate: ''trite [[law]]''.
#* '''2017''', Ontario Superior Court of Justice, [http://canlii.ca/t/h3g10 ''Taucar v Human Rights Tribunal of Ontario'', 2017 ONSC 2604]:
#*: It is '''trite''' to say that the mere fact that a decision does not favour the applicant or that the applicant disagrees with the decision does not establish that the decision is tainted with bias.

